        "introduction": "Hash-based collections form the operational core of Java enterprise services. In this lesson on **Equals and HashCode Contract Violations in HashMaps**, we inspect hash distributions, bucket treeification, and concurrency mechanics.",
        "definition": "**Equals and HashCode Contract Violations in HashMaps** defines the hash table architecture in java.util.HashMap that maps keys to values with amortized O(1) performance using array buckets and Red-Black tree conversion.",
        "explanation": "Internal mechanics of **Equals and HashCode Contract Violations in HashMaps**:\n1. **Hashing Function:** Applies `key.hashCode() ^ (h >>> 16)` to spread higher bits into lower bits and reduce bucket collisions.\n2. **Bucket Indexing:** Calculates target index via bitwise masking: `(n - 1) & hash`, requiring capacity to always be a power of two.\n3. **Collision Chaining & Treeification:** Collisions chain nodes in a linked list; when a bucket exceeds 8 nodes and total capacity is >= 64, it treeifies into a Red-Black Tree (TreeNode) for O(log n) worst-case lookup.\n4. **Resizing:** When size exceeds `capacity * loadFactor` (default 16 * 0.75 = 12), capacity doubles and keys rehash via low/high bit checks.",
        "importantConcepts": [
          "Default initial capacity is 16; default load factor is 0.75.",
          "Treeification threshold is 8 nodes; untreeification threshold is 6 nodes.",
          "HashMap allows one null key and multiple null values; ConcurrentHashMap forbids nulls.",
          "ConcurrentHashMap utilizes synchronized bucket heads and CAS operations for thread safety."
        ],
