        "introduction": "Functional-style declarative processing transforms data transformation pipelines in modern Java. In this lesson on **Circuit Breaker Pattern: Handling Cascading Downstream Failures**, we explore Stream pipelines, lazy evaluation, and terminal operations.",
        "definition": "**Circuit Breaker Pattern: Handling Cascading Downstream Failures** defines the declarative functional processing pipeline in java.util.stream that processes sequences of elements through intermediate transformations and terminal reductions.",
        "explanation": "Core mechanics of **Circuit Breaker Pattern: Handling Cascading Downstream Failures**:\n1. **Pipeline Architecture:** A Stream pipeline consists of a Source (Collection, array), zero or more Intermediate operations, and exactly one Terminal operation.\n2. **Lazy Evaluation:** Intermediate operations (`filter`, `map`, `sorted`) are not executed immediately; they form an execution recipe evaluated only when a terminal operation is called.\n3. **Short-Circuiting:** Operations like `findFirst()`, `anyMatch()`, and `limit()` terminate processing as soon as a condition is satisfied without iterating the entire dataset.\n4. **Immutability:** Streams do not alter the underlying data source; they yield new values or collect into new immutable data structures.",
        "importantConcepts": [
          "Intermediate operations return a new Stream and are always lazy.",
          "Terminal operations (collect, reduce, count, forEach) trigger pipeline execution and consume the stream.",
          "A Stream cannot be reused once a terminal operation has executed.",
          "Optional<T> prevents NullPointerExceptions by modeling the presence or absence of a value."
        ],
        "syntax": "List<String> results = list.stream()\n    .filter(s -> !s.isBlank())\n    .map(String::toUpperCase)\n    .collect(Collectors.toList());",
